/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_setup Setup of ExaGRyPE

The ExaGRyPE code is organized by a main Python script (i.e., ccz4.py), which we refer to as the Python API. By calling this script, Peano will automatically create the project and generate the actual C++ code according to the user’s setup, and then compile the final executable. We integrate most of the tunable features and settings in the API script, thus users can easily control the behaviour of their simulations by modifying the single script and corresponding calling command. There is also some setup in the C++ files like \c CCZ4.cpp or \c InitialValues.cpp .

Below, we provide a brief introduction to the important options provided by ExaGRyPE. One can find useful information by typing \c --help for explanation on the parameters that can be controlled directly by the calling command. Most of them should be self-explanatory as well.

# Solvers

The solver, which is responsible for the exact numerical scheme that updates cells, can be picked by specifying the \c args.implementation arguments. There are multiple default solver options that users can use, and for the vacuum simulation in ExaGRyPE, the \c fd4-rk1-dsl solver is recommended. This solver stands for a fourth-order finite differences + Euler time integration with an adaptive time stepping that adjusts step size according to the system eigenvalue. This solver balances the accuracy and performance; most of the results in the release paper of ExaGRyPE are produced with this solver. A higher RK scheme gives a better time accuracy but with a higher computational burden. The \c -dsl suffix indicates a solver that was generated by DSL (domain-specific language).

Also, we recommend using the second order formulation (notice it is not the order of FD accuracy) by adding \c -so flag to your calling command, as the first order formulation would give some unexpected fluctuation behaviour in static scenarios like the single Schwarzschild black hole.

The other solvers are not recommended in the vacuum scenario simulation, including the outdated ones that no longer get maintained (e.g., with suffix \c -adaptive) or the finite volume type solver that yields a high diffusion.

# Scenarios

The main testing scenarios in ExaGRyPE are given in the previous page, which include the gauge wave, single black hole(SBH), and rotating binary black holes(BBHs). While different scenarios can be switched directly via calling command, the exact parameters (e.g., the amplitude of the gauge wave, the mass of the black holes) need to be set in the corresponding C++ files (\c InitialValues.cpp). We recommend users always start with the gauge wave benchmark, as it serves as a good starting point to familiarize themselves with how the code is organized. For the black hole spacetime, we import the \c TwoPuncture module from EinsteinToolkit as an external library. If users plan to import their own external library for the initial condition, the binding code for the TwoPuncture module in the \c External directory should be a useful reference.

# Tracer(Particles)

See \ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_advanced

# Interpolation and Restriction

ExaGRyPE provides several strategies on how the solution along the resolution transition boundary is interpolated(restricted), with different accuracy and computation burden. The updated strategies \c matrix, which is much faster compared to the legacy ones, while keeping the linear accuracy. The high-order strategy (\c second-order) is slightly more expensive but yields a much better transition along the boundary, thus we recommmend to enable this one during the simulation involving black holes (SBH or BBHs case). For more information on the strategy, one can refer to <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15814">this paper</a>.

# Checkpoint and Restarting

See \ref page_exahype_checkpointing


*/

